 ## Overview
-Classification and transfer functions map volumetric data values (and derived features) to optical properties—primarily color and opacity—to determine which structures are visible and how they appear in the final image [S1, S2, S3, unofficial][S4]. This process solves the problem of visual ambiguity in 3D data by allowing users or algorithms to emphasize specific materials while suppressing irrelevant background noise [S2, unofficial][S4]. Unlike sibling methods that focus on multi-field visualization, illustrative rendering styles, or interactive widget placement, this family focuses specifically on the mathematical and algorithmic mapping from data space to visual space [39-multi-field-vector-and-tensor-volume-visualization.md], [40-illustrative-and-non-photorealistic-volume-rendering.md], [41-interaction.md].
