-### Pre-classification
-A classification approach where the transfer function (TF) is applied to scalar values at the grid points before sampling interpolation. Color and opacity are assigned at the resampling points [S8]. This approach suppresses high-frequency information introduced by nonlinear TFs, causing the rendered image to appear blurry [S8]. Choose this when anti-aliasing and smoothness are prioritized over sharp edge definition.
+### [Pre-classification](6103-pre-classification.md)
+A classification approach where the transfer function (TF) is applied to scalar values at the grid points before sampling interpolation [6103-pre-classification.md]. Color and opacity are assigned at the resampling points [6103-pre-classification.md]. This approach suppresses high-frequency information introduced by nonlinear TFs, causing the rendered image to appear blurry. Choose this when anti-aliasing and smoothness are prioritized over sharp edge definition [6103-pre-classification.md].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of transfer functions in volume rendering originated with the need to map scalar data to optical properties for visualization [S1, S2, unofficial]. Early implementations used lookup tables and piecewise linear functions [S5][S3, unofficial]. The distinction between pre-classification and post-classification was formalized to address artifacts such as blurring and aliasing [S2, unofficial][S8]. The introduction of GPU hardware accelerated the evaluation of transfer functions, enabling real-time interaction [1551-real-time-transfer-function-evaluation.md]. Recent developments focus on automated and learning-based methods to reduce the manual effort required to design effective transfer functions [5933-transfer-function-optimization-and-learning.md].
